<windowex:WindowEx
    x:Class="MTM_Waitlist.MainWindow"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:local="using:MTM_Waitlist"
    xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
    xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
    xmlns:windowex="using:WinUIEx"
    MinWidth="500"
    MinHeight="500"
    mc:Ignorable="d">
    <Window.SystemBackdrop>
        <MicaBackdrop/>
    </Window.SystemBackdrop>

    <!--
        The one window for the whole life of the application (`contracts/window-stage-contract.md` §1; FR-016,
        FR-019, FR-043). The host assigns the stage it is showing to the presenter below: the launch stages while
        the launch runs, and the shell once the launch hands over. Nothing else ever fills it, and the navigation
        panel exists only inside the shell stage, so nothing in the application can assume a signed-in person
        before the hand-over (FR-023).

        The presenter is why the window declares content of its own: the stage content is swapped into it rather
        than assigned to the window, so the window's own root element — which the theme and the dialogs are
        applied to — survives every swap.

        There is deliberately no `PersistenceId`: a window whose placement is restored between sessions would
        come back maximized from the previous run's shell and could not then open centred at the launch stage's
        size (FR-020, FR-021).
    -->
    <Grid x:Name="RootGrid">
        <ContentPresenter
            x:Name="StageHost"
            HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
            VerticalAlignment="Stretch" />
    </Grid>
</windowex:WindowEx>
